WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.127
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi.

00:00:01.127 --> 00:00:03.030
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.740
of "Deconstructing
Chatbots," we will

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.910
continue to help the pulse team
extend their single-flow agent

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.360
into a multi-flow agent that
can handle not just orders

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.980
but also returns.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.490
We will also see how they can
manage multiple agent versions

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.110
and deploy to
different environments.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.392
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.040
Before we dive into the console,
let's lay out the Use Case

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.480
requirements first.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.540
Most of Puggs's customer
requests are of three types--

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.940
orders, returns, and exchanges.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.970
Each of these requests have
their own conversational twists

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.540
and turns and three different
teams that manage those.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.840
We recommend Puggs to
create three separate flows

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.280
for these requests.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.680
Multiple flows allows
different teams

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.430
to be responsible for building
and maintaining those flows.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.160
And they provide better
conversational control,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.320
help scale quickly,
and they don't

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.690
incur any additional cost.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.230
We already created the order
flow in the previous episode.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.690
If you haven't watched
it, it is linked below.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.540
Stop and watch that one
before continuing here,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.470
because this is going to be
the extension of that episode.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.410
We will be creating
a return flow

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.590
so Puggs can take return
requests through the Virtual

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:21.400
Agent.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.130
We will keep the flow
simple by just collecting

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.310
the order number and providing
a return confirmation.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.580
So now that we know
what we are building,

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.310
let's head to the
Dialogflow CX console.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.950
Let's continue working
in our Puggs project.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.490
Add two flows to it for
return and exchange.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.830
In our return flow
at the start page,

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:45.520
we define our transition
route to order.return intent,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.580
which will understand
the user's intent.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.070
Add a few examples
of training phrases,

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:53.140
such as I need a refund.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.390
I would like to return my order.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.520
Add a few more along
the same lines.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.530
And before saving this, let's
add the transition to a page

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.990
to collect return information.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.320
In this page, let's ask the
user for the order number.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.509
I've provided a
few examples here.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:13.420
To process a return, we
need the order number,

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.040
so we will make that as
a required parameter.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.720
And for now, let's just
select a system entity number,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.240
although in real life,
Puggs would probably

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.090
have a custom entity with a
certain sequence of numbers

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.720
that would be matched to their
back end through the web hub.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.780
At this state, we check
if the order number is

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.030
received by adding
the condition,

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.210
page.params.status equals FINAL.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:40.390
Once the condition is
met, we want to transition

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.780
to another page to provide
a return confirmation.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.510
At the return confirmation
page, all we do

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.990
is send a confirmation saying
your return is processed,

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.480
adding our session
parameter order number

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.790
in there using the dollar sign.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.400
And we transition from
here to the end page.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.000
Although we could transition
to another flow as well if you

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.230
wanted the user to, say, take
a survey or order more things.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.780
There's a lot more that
you can do in returns,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.990
you could add more
parameters to collect,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.950
connect to the
Puggs' back in API.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.930
But I will leave that
to you to explore,

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.140
since the point of this video is
to get you familiar with the CX

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.330
console and the concepts.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.690
Now, we know that Puggs
has multiple teams working

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:23.740
on the flows.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.260
They need the ability
to save progress

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.630
by creating different
versions and then

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:31.150
deploy specific
versions of their flows

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:35.030
to development, testing,
or production environments.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.870
Let me show you how versions
and environments work

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.340
in Puggs agent.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:43.540
We go to the Manage tab and in
deployment we find versions.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.660
Now here, since Puggs
has multiple flows,

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.070
each flow can have
its own version

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.630
so the teams can independently
manage their own versions.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:56.110
Versions are snapshots
of the flow data

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.190
and associated agent data like
intents, entities, web posts,

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.130
pages, [INAUDIBLE]
groups, and a lot more.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.160
Now, if we click on
a flow here, let's

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.770
say return, create a version.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.860
Give it a name and description
and give it a few seconds

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.060
to become ready.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:15.320
And if we want to
use this version,

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.560
then we can just load
it in our drafts load

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.970
by clicking on this button.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.910
But let's make another
version so we can actually

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.380
see these changes.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.790
Say in this new version, we made
a change to our entry dialog.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.240
Then, go to the versions
and load a previous version.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.970
We see that the change
we made is not there

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.300
anymore because we are now
working in an older version.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.550
So that's how
versioning works in CX.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.250
Now as a best practice,
Puggs obviously

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:48.100
wants to separate their
production and development

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.970
environments to make sure the
development can keep going

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:54.220
while production is stable.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.220
To do that, we go to
environments and create

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.540
a new environment.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.550
Let's say production
in this case.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.520
And we select the corresponding
flow versions that

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.710
should be used in production.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:09.970
We can do the same to set up
dev and test environments too.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.550
Now, once environments
are set up,

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.940
make sure you are testing
things in the right environment.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.550
In the simulator, you can
select the environment

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.650
and the corresponding flow.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.160
If you're using the API,
pass the environment ID

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.260
in the URL like this.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.050
Otherwise, it uses the
default environment,

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.940
which is your draft flow.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:31.600
All right.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.690
With that, we have
created a multi-flow agent

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:38.080
for Puggs's team that
takes orders and returns.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.690
We also learned how
versions and environments

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:42.760
work in Dialogflow CX.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:44.260
Now, it's your turn.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.260
Design the third exchange
flow for Puggs's team

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:50.050
and share your experience
